.TH CHMAP_INSERT_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_insert_elem \- insert a key-value pair into the hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_insert_elem(chmap chm,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts a key-value pair into the hash map. If the key already exists,
the new value replaces the old one. The map resizes automatically when
its load factor crosses a threshold.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to insert into
key_pair   Key to insert (copied into map)
val_pair   Value to associate with key (copied into map)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success               - New key inserted successfully
ccol_key_already_present   - Key already existed; value updated successfully
ccol_invalid_args          - chm, key_pair, or val_pair is NULL or size is 0;
                             also returned when key_pair->size does not
                             exactly match the byte size of the key type,
                             for any fixed-width numeric key type (char,
                             short, int, long, long long, their unsigned
                             counterparts, float, double, long double, or
                             a pointer type), regardless of which backend
                             the map uses; also returned when
                             val_pair->size does not exactly match the byte
                             size of the value type the map was created
                             with, for any fixed-width value type, and
                             regardless of which backend the map uses. A
                             value type with no fixed width (ccol_string
                             and ccol_other_types) is never checked,
                             because every entry of such a map
                             legitimately carries its own size
ccol_container_full        - Map has reached ccol_max_elem_count
ccol_not_enough_memory     - Memory allocation failed
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no specified alignment, because the map
hashes and compares the key bytes through memcpy instead of through a
pointer cast to the key type. key_pair->ptr can therefore point directly
at key bytes at any offset in a larger buffer, such as a field in a
packed network frame or a memory-mapped record, and you do not have to
copy the key bytes into an aligned local variable first
Average O(1) complexity
The map copies the key data and the value data into itself
The map frees and replaces the old value when the key is already there
The map resizes itself when the load factor goes above its threshold.
With open addressing, an insert of a new key doubles the table once the
live entries fill more than 0.70 of it; a delete leaves no marker for
a deleted slot, so only live entries count
A value of the wrong width is rejected at the INSERT rather than at a
later read. Without that check the insert would report success, and the
value-size guard of chmap_get(3) would stop the process on the first
read of that key, at a call site that did nothing wrong
The type-inferred chmap_insert(3) macro cannot reach that rejection,
because it first converts the value through the declared value type of
the map, so every pair that it builds is exactly the right width
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
A map created with a custom key equality procedure compares keys with
it; see chmap_create_full(3)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_insert (3),
.BR chmap_delete_elem (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
